>
> 1. In terms of reformulating the model to be in terms of logit(p), I have a large number of trials in which p=1, and logit(p) = inf.  Do you have any suggestions about how I should handle those trials?  There are many of them, and I would prefer to not exclude them.

But aren’t the p unobserved?  If they’re parameters then unless the data are all 0’s and 1’s
then the latent p would never quite reach 0 or 1.  logit(p), which can get very close to 0 and 1
would the be okay.

If there are some latent p that are known then you’ll have to use the nominal scale between 0
and 1.  But then if you convolve those known p to get some other latent, unobserved p (as in
those individual belief p’s) then you’ll have to be careful to ensure that you stay within those
boundaries.

> 2. For k[i] ~ binomial_logit(N[i], logit_p_obs[i]), what is N[i]?  I couldn't find this in the manual/documentation.  Does it correspond to that particular trial number?  Or the total number of trials?

Total number of trials.  If you have N trials with probability of success p, then
the number of successes is distributed as

k ~ binomial(N, p);

Or

k ~ binomial_logit(N, logit(p));

