On Thursday, July 7, 2016 at 3:24:19 PM UTC-4, Ben Lambert wrote:
> Hi,
>
> I have estimated a Hidden Markov Model (HMM) for animal movement states (with the help of Bob! See the attached.) The marginalisation of the latent states is handled via dynamic programming using the forwards/backwards algorithm.
>
> I have a number of model permutations which I would like to compare using some measure of predictive performance. For example, I would like to compare a two-state HMM with a three-state one.
>

Just a word of caution about predictions in
this context.  Lots of time-series models will produce decent
single time-step predictions just because they are set up as
stepwise models.  To really see if you have a decent model
you need multi-step predictions on a meaningful time scale.

In other words, I can tell you where that elk is going to be
fifteen minutes after the last GPS fix, it'll be near where you
last saw it, and if you can't beat that model (y[t]=y[t-1]) an
hour or two out you might want to rethink your modeling approach.

With infectious disease count time series we've been taking data
like this and partitioning it into meaningful blocks to do cross validation (since
dropping a single observation doesn't make much sense anyway)
but we've also taken an iterative approach where for (almost) each
t* you predict y[t>t*] conditional on y[t<=t*].  This mimics how data
would arrive and lets you look at prediction quality as a function
of t-t*.  It's a good comparison to make against models like AR(1)
and y[t]=y[t-1] to see if you can do better.

Krzysztof

> My question is - does anyone have any ideas how to estimate WAIC (or LOO-CV) without repeatedly partitioning the data into T-1 points and a single datum? The model takes about half an hour to estimate for ~18,000 data points, and so doing this for all points is clearly not feasible.
>
> Best,
>
> Ben

