> On Jul 8, 2016, at 10:28 AM, Krzysztof Sakrejda <krzysztof...@gmail.com> wrote:

...

> Just a word of caution about predictions in
> this context.  Lots of time-series models will produce decent
> single time-step predictions just because they are set up as
> stepwise models.  To really see if you have a decent model
> you need multi-step predictions on a meaningful time scale.

Do you mean predicting the entire rest of the series, like

  p(y[(t+1):T] | y[1:t])

?

> In other words, I can tell you where that elk is going to be
> fifteen minutes after the last GPS fix, it'll be near where you
> last saw it, and if you can't beat that model (y[t]=y[t-1]) an
> hour or two out you might want to rethink your modeling approach.

It's a little more subtle than that.  The elk tend to either
mill about, or walk in a straightish line.  So it's not just
a random diffusion around where they're at for each time step.

But then when you zoom out scales, it does look like a diffusion
again.   Without longer-scale history data, say for fixed migrations
paths, or covariates, such as location of food and water and terrain
features, it's hard to tell where an animal's going to go.

The real problem is that y[t] = y[t-1] isn't a model.  It doesn't provide
a density for p(y[t] | y[t-1]).  So how do I evaluate against a non-model?
With mean square error or something?  In the mixture HMM case, there are two
components, change in heading and distance moved---together with the current
bearing and state (transiting, foraging, sleeping, etc.), they give you a
prediction of where you'll be next.

- Bob

