Well, I want to use log loss to compare probabilistic predictions.
That is, I want to lay out an area and ask what the probability
is that the animal will be in it.  Or at least evaluate the density
of the point the animal's actually at.

> for infectious disease
> we've done mean absolute error and relative mean absolute error  (MAE1/MAEref)
> where MAEref is for some reference model (sometimes as simple as y[t]=y[t-1]). Typically
> we evaluate these as a function of prediction horizon or something like that.

But I want to do full Bayes for my predictions.  I'd integrate
the error measure over the posterior predictive to derive an
expected error and bounds:

INT err(y[(t+1):T], y'[(t+1):T]) p(y'[(t+1):T] | y[1:t]) dy'.

