> > > you need multi-step predictions on a meaningful time scale.
> >
> > Do you mean predicting the entire rest of the series, like
> >
> >   p(y[(t+1):T] | y[1:t])
> >
> > ?
> >
> > Yes.
> >
> >
> > > In other words, I can tell you where that elk is going to be
> > > fifteen minutes after the last GPS fix, it'll be near where you
> > > last saw it, and if you can't beat that model (y[t]=y[t-1]) an
> > > hour or two out you might want to rethink your modeling approach.
> >
> > It's a little more subtle than that.  The elk tend to either
> > mill about, or walk in a straightish line.  So it's not just
> > a random diffusion around where they're at for each time step.
> >
> >
> > Yes, so after a basic exploratory look at the data you are suggesting
> > that a two-state model of this sort will do better than random diffusion
> > at predicting animal movement.  Fine, I'm saying test that.
>
> That is, of course, what the ecologists do.  In lots of ways,
> but mainly with BIC.
>
> So what I'm asking is, what's the baseline?  It can't just be
> a point mass.  I suppose it could be a uniform turning angle
> and then some fitted distance moved?
>
> > Additionally,
> > if you're going to talk about modeling animal movement test it more
> > than one time step ahead and see how far ahead your generative model
> > gets you. In general you'll do better than diffusion on short term
> > time scales and then worse again and the real question is how big that
> > window is.
>
> But I'd think if the model's reasonable, it'll do better at longer
> time scales, too, than a simpler model.

If your more complex model does better than a simpler model at
shorter and longer time scales I think that's a desirable property but
you don't get it for free.  In ecology there's a nice series of articles about
this between Sugihara and Hartig that's unfortunately couched as
parametric versus non-parametric models.

> Even though it won't be able to
> make an accurate prediction, it should be able to estimate the density
> of everywhere it might be.  The correlation among bearings will mean
> that you can explain longer walks ---it's a kind of overdispersion.
>
> > The real problem is that y[t] = y[t-1] isn't a model.  It doesn't provide
> > a density for p(y[t] | y[t-1]).  So how do I evaluate against a non-model?
> > With mean square error or something?  In the mixture HMM case, there are two
> > components, change in heading and distance moved---together with the current
> > bearing and state (transiting, foraging, sleeping, etc.), they give you a
> > prediction of where you'll be next.
> >
> > Sure it's a point prediction.  You can use any error you want,
>
> Well, I want to use log loss to compare probabilistic predictions.
> That is, I want to lay out an area and ask what the probability
> is that the animal will be in it.  Or at least evaluate the density
> of the point the animal's actually at.

Yeah, fair enough, for that particular approach something like AR(1)
(for infectious disease) or diffusion (for animal movement) would
be a better comparison.  For longer time scales I don't think these
HMM's are going to do much better than diffusion.  The length of
the window where they do better is what's interesting.

>
> > for infectious disease
> > we've done mean absolute error and relative mean absolute error  (MAE1/MAEref)
> > where MAEref is for some reference model (sometimes as simple as y[t]=y[t-1]). Typically
> > we evaluate these as a function of prediction horizon or something like that.
>
> But I want to do full Bayes for my predictions.  I'd integrate
> the error measure over the posterior predictive to derive an
> expected error and bounds:
>
> INT err(y[(t+1):T], y'[(t+1):T]) p(y'[(t+1):T] | y[1:t]) dy'.

I think we're on the same page here, except that you're really against doing
comparisons to point mass models because you can't do this integration.
That's fine, there are other time series reference models that you can do
this integration with (AR(1) or diffusion).  My main point was that it's almost always
 interesting to do the comparison and to also check how far into the future
a fancy model does better than a reference model.

Krzysztof

>
> - Bob
