I think you're right, because on a long enough time scale, a
mixture of resting, foraging, and directed movement is going to
look like a diffusion of some sort.  At shorter scales, though,
they can manage some level of directedness to movement and account
for the mixture of consistent shorter-range and longer-range
movements.  Most of the focus on longer-range movement seemed to
be on covariates (where is there food, where are there hills, where
other fish or whales or panthers are) and constraints (dolphins
can't move across land), or they just directly estimated something
like a point process.

