For the variable that is continuous but you only measured it at 2 levels, I don't believe that there's any benefit in treating it as a second continuous dimension in the GP; indeed, it may even be computationally wasteful. My understanding is that with only 2 measurement points, you'll only ever find support for at max a linear effect anyway. So, really you have a single-dimension GP measured at 6 points crossed with two 2-level discrete variables.

On Thu, Jul 14, 2016 at 11:18 AM, Linas Mockus <linasm...@gmail.com> wrote:

> In this case there is bunch of data: 2X6X2 where first covariate is discrete and has two levels, while second and third covariates are continuous and have 6 and 2 levels correspondingly. Unfortunately design is unbalanced. In addition each set of covariates has 50 replicates - total 800 data points.
>
> I am curious if encoding of discrete covariates matters: 0, 1 vs. -1, 1. Maybe scaling continuous covariates could help?
>
> Thanks,
> Linas

