Hello,

  I am trying to fit a linear meta-regression model to a relatively small data set (154 observations with four categorical predictors).  When I fit the model in rstan, I consistently receive the warning "There were X divergent transitions after warmup" for values of X typically between 1 and 10.  Following advice from other posts on this topic, I have increased adapt_delta to 0.999, decreased stepsize to 0.001, and increased max_treedepth to 18.  I have also made the priors more informative.  The current Stan model is:

data{

   int<lower=1> N;

   int<lower=1> nAs;

   int<lower=1> nBs;

   int<lower=1> nCs;

   int<lower=1> nDs;

   int<lower=1, upper=nAs> A[N];

   int<lower=1, upper=nBs> B[N];

   int<lower=1, upper=nCs> C[N];

   int<lower=1, upper=nDs> D[N];

   vector[N] y;

   vector[N] y_se;

}

parameters{

   real alpha;

   vector[nAs] a_raw;

   vector[nBs] b_raw;

   vector[nCs] c_raw;

   vector[nDs] d_raw;

   real<lower=0> sigma_a;

   real<lower=0> sigma_b;

   real<lower=0> sigma_c;

   real<lower=0> sigma_d;

}

transformed parameters{

   vector[nAs] a;

   vector[nBs] b;

   vector[nCs] c;

   vector[nDs] d;

   a <- a_raw*sigma_a;

   b <- b_raw*sigma_b;

   c <- c_raw*sigma_c;

   d <- d_raw*sigma_d;

}

model{

  real yHat[N];

  for(i in 1:N){

     yHat[i] <- alpha + a[A[i]] + b[B[i]] + c[C[i]] + d[D[i]];

    }

  y ~ normal(yHat, y_se);

  alpha ~ normal(0, 10);

  a_raw ~ normal(0, 1);

  b_raw ~ normal(0, 1);

  c_raw ~ normal(0, 1);

  d_raw ~ normal(0, 1);

  sigma_a ~ cauchy(0, 2);

  sigma_b ~ cauchy(0, 2);

  sigma_c ~ cauchy(0, 2);

  sigma_d ~ cauchy(0, 2);

}

The file, accompanying R code, and (pseudo) data are attached.  I will be grateful for any suggestions on how to re-specify or re-parametrize this model to avoid the divergent transitions.

While attempting to resolve this issue, I have noticed that the number of divergent transitions may change dramatically from one run to the next, even if I don't change the code at all.   I'm curious about how others approach this question from a reproducibility and modeling robustness standpoint; if the model ever produces divergent transitions, should I assume it's mis-specified and revise accordingly?

Gratefully yours,

Lauren

