Thank you!  Your comment on the number of random effects reminded my collaborator of a solution for scenarios where we wish to model several categorical variables, each with comparatively few levels, as in this case: to borrow strength across the variance components.  We did so, following the recommendation in Gelman and Hill (p. 500), which eliminated the divergent transitions.  The final code is attached and below.

Gratefully yours,
Lauren

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

data{
   int<lower=1> N;
   int<lower=1> nAs;
   int<lower=1> nBs;
   int<lower=1> nCs;
   int<lower=1> nDs;

   int<lower=1, upper=nAs> A[N];
   int<lower=1, upper=nBs> B[N];
   int<lower=1, upper=nCs> C[N];
   int<lower=1, upper=nDs> D[N];
   vector[N] y;
   vector[N] y_se;

}

parameters{

   real alpha;
   vector[nAs] a_raw;
   vector[nBs] b_raw;
   vector[nCs] c_raw;
   vector[nDs] d_raw;

   real<lower=0> sigma_a;
   real<lower=0> sigma_b;
   real<lower=0> sigma_c;
   real<lower=0> sigma_d;

   real<lower=0> sigma_epsilon;
   real<lower = 0, upper = 10> phi;

}

transformed parameters{

   vector[nAs] a;
   vector[nBs] b;
   vector[nCs] c;
   vector[nDs] d;

   a <- a_raw*sigma_a;
   b <- b_raw*sigma_b;
   c <- c_raw*sigma_c;
   d <- d_raw*sigma_d;
}

model{
  real yHat[N];

  real ySd[N];

  for(i in 1:N){
     yHat[i] <- alpha + a[A[i]] + b[B[i]] + c[C[i]] + d[D[i]];

     ySd[i] <- sqrt(pow(y_se[i], 2) + pow(sigma_epsilon, 2));
    }

  y ~ normal(yHat, ySd);

  alpha ~ normal(0, 10);
  a_raw ~ normal(0, 1);
  b_raw ~ normal(0, 1);
  c_raw ~ normal(0, 1);
  d_raw ~ normal(0, 1);

  sigma_a ~ cauchy(0, phi);
  sigma_b ~ cauchy(0, phi);
  sigma_c ~ cauchy(0, phi);
  sigma_d ~ cauchy(0, phi);
  sigma_epsilon ~ cauchy(0, phi);
}

On Friday, July 15, 2016 at 8:53:52 AM UTC-4, Michael Betancourt wrote:
> Just a quick note —divergences simply indicate that Stan cannot fit the model, it does
> not necessarily imply that the model is incompatible with the data (although there’s often
> a strong correlation, which is the basis of the “Folk Theorem”).  But it’s important to take
> divergences seriously, because if you’re not getting an accurate fit then you can’t really
> judge the compatibility of your model!
>
> Upon a very brief look your model looks appropriate — but perhaps there might be too
> many random effects and what you are seeing is a consequently of extreme linear
> correlations (as opposed to the nonlinear correlations that we typical worry about in
> these hierarchical models).
>
> On Jul 15, 2016, at 8:24 AM, Lauren Vollmer <vollmer...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> > I'm curious about how others approach this question from a reproducibility and modeling robustness standpoint; if the model ever produces divergent transitions, should I assume it's mis-specified and revise accordingly?
> >
>

