Subject: Re: [stan-users] Reparameterisation of multivariate normal with unknown parameters and observed data
Message-ID: <lJDEpy71iYU.93JS5rY_AwAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <lJDEpy71iYU.FYAfzMOgAgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <lJDEpy71iYU.FYAfzMOgAgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/lJDEpy71iYU/m/93JS5rY_AwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

No, you don't need a Jacobian adjustment when you transform
parameters and use them as parameters (rather than as variables).
For example:

parameters {
  real<lower=0> alpha;
transformed parameters {
  real<lower=0> inv_alpha;
  inv_alpha = 1 / alpha;
model {
  y ~ bar(inv_alpha);      // no Jacobian needed
  inv_alpha ~ foo(theta);  // require log Jacobian of 1/alpha transform

Numerically, you almost never want to apply inv(Sigma) to
a matrix.  And you never ever want to do

  inv(a) * b

Instead, the above should be written as

  a \ b

It's more efficient and more stable numerically.  We're going to
start trying to add automatic warnings to this effect to the parser
in the future.

- Bob

