Hi Bob

Thanks for the reply. I looked at some of the examples in the manual, but I am still a bit lost. I am not
sure how to parametrise p(y | k)  (where y is the new test sample) in the model block, or am I missing something and
this should be a generated quantity? For something like linear regression you can parametrise the mean of a normal, as in plug in
the new test sample x_new:  y_new ~ normal(x_new * beta, sigma); and declare y_new as a parameter.

But I can't do something similar in my case?

Sorry if it's really a stupid question, I have been trawling the manual and the mailing list for a while, maybe I am just missing the right keywords.

Regards
Karl

On Wednesday, July 20, 2016 at 10:04:54 PM UTC+2, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> Stan will do this all for you pretty much automatically.
> The manual has some examples of how to do the predictive
> distributions within Stan (that is, you use MCMC for
> the integral).
>
> As to the normalized category probabilities, you just
> need to normalize:
>
>   vector[K] lp;
>   simplex[K] log_theta;
>
>   log_theta = lp - log_sum_exp(lp);
>
> This assumes lp[k] = p(y | k) * p(k),
> where p(y|k) is probably of observation given category k
> and p(k) is the marginal probability of categories.
>
> Then log_theta[k] is log Pr[C = k | y].
>
>   exp(theta) = exp(lp) / sum(exp(lp))
>
> - Bob
