Hi

Is this what you had in mind:

generated quantities{
  vector[M] lp;
  vector[M] log_theta;  //should not be a simplex, log probabilities don't sum to 1
  simplex[M] theta; // P(C = m | x_new)

  for (m in 1:M)

      lp[m] <- 1/M + normal_log(x_new, mu[m], sigma[m]); //just assuming the class priors are 1/M

  for (m in 1:M)

      log_theta[m] <- lp[m] - log_sum_exp(lp);

  theta <- exp(log_theta);

}

I don't see how this can be done as part of the model block (do I even want that?) So here I get a probability distribution P(C = m | x_new) and then the expected value of
that distribution with a loss function (which I can't do in Stan, but in Python or R) gives me a single number?

Regards
Karl

