Subject: How can I avoid exponentiating a vector by a vector?
Message-ID: <uS0mUadacrs.KTeXA_y4BQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/uS0mUadacrs/m/KTeXA_y4BQAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello,

As a disclaimer, this is the first model I've written with Stan.

I'm having difficulty overcoming the exponentiation of a vector by a vector, as seen in line 64 of the attached script:

effort = B * ((ScaledEffort ^ p) - 1) / p;

which yields the following error: "arguments to ^ must be primitive (real or int); cannot exponentiate vector by vector".

I've tried converting this to:

for(i in 1:ncounts) {
 effort[i] =
   B[regions[i]] * ((ScaledEffort[i] ^ p[regions[i]]) - 1) / p[regions[i]];
}

to force element-wise exponentiation (as suggested in this discussion <https://groups.google.com/forum/#!msg/stan-users/Q7zADp0Ho9E/gF9SHDpmvBQJ>), but that yielded this error: "Exception thrown at line 92: stan::math::normal_log: Location parameter has dimension = 1, expecting dimension = 3; a function was called with arguments of different scalar, array, vector, or matrix types, and they were not consistently sized;  all arguments must be scalars or multidimensional values of the same shape."

This new error refers to this line:

B ~ normal(B_mu, B_sigma);

which apparently now has only dimension=1 instead of dimension=3 (corresponding to the number of regions across which the data are spread).

Any advice about maneuvering around this would be much appreciated.

Kevin

